* Round 7 — pjb pressed on the low-level layer's robustness, 2026-09-11

pjb's framing: distinguish the low-level OSA/keystroke layer from the
alfe runtime/protocol layer above it (proven by --mode batch, which
shares that code). Pointed questions: while typing a long `(load "...")`
(a full runLspFile path can be 100+ characters -> 12+ keyDown bursts),
does this detect a WINDOW change mid-injection (not just a different app
becoming frontmost)? Does it read the screen to confirm before RET? Does
it resume typing after regaining focus, or restart?

** Gap found on inspection (before any live test): the Round 6
CONSOLESTILLFOCUSED-equivalent check only tested \"is bricscad the
frontmost PROCESS\" — true even when the user (or BricsCAD itself)
switches KEY WINDOW from the console to the drawing window WITHOUT
switching app. Confirmed live that the right primitive exists:
`tell process \"bricscad\" to get value of attribute \"AXFocusedWindow\"'
correctly reports the console while it holds focus.

** Fixes implemented (autolisp-front-end/source/backend-bricscad.lisp):

  1. CONSOLESTILLFOCUSED now checks the CONJUNCTION of \"bricscad is the
     frontmost process\" AND \"the console is bricscad's own
     AXFocusedWindow\" — catches a same-app window-focus change, not
     only a different app stealing focus outright.
  2. VERIFYTYPEDLINE: a genuine CONTENT-level check before committing
     with Return. The console has no AXValue, so pixel OCR looked like
     the only alternative — but a live round-trip proved the widget
     supports ordinary text selection and Cmd+C: typing a marker string,
     Shift+Home, Cmd+C, reading `the clipboard' back gave the prompt +
     the typed form back verbatim. Matched by suffix (the prompt text is
     not ours to hard-code).
  3. RECOVERCONSOLE reordered: re-activate + re-focus the console BEFORE
     sending Escape. The Round 6 version sent Escape FIRST, which (on a
     genuine deviation) would reach whatever OTHER window was still key,
     not the console — never actually clearing the partial fragment it
     was meant to discard. Own bug, found re-reading the Round 6 code
     rather than by a fresh live failure.
  4. Dialog handling hardened: `display dialog' itself raises -128
     (\"User canceled\") when ITS \"Cancel\" button is clicked OR when
     Escape/Cmd-period is pressed while it is showing — it does not
     return a normal record in that case. That native -128 is now caught
     explicitly and converted into this script's own clear, numbered
     error, so a decline is never a bare, ambiguous -128 again — found
     necessary live: pjb reported clicking \"Retry\", the run still ended
     in a raw -128, and there was no way from that alone to tell whether
     the click was actually registered as Cancel, whether Escape had been
     pressed, or whether this was a genuine bug.

** New bug found ONLY by then running the (updated) template live:
CLEARCONSOLELINE added (End, Shift+Home, Backspace), now run before
typing on EVERY attempt, not only after a detected deviation. The
console turns out to be a real position-aware text field, not an
append-only terminal: a click can land MID-STRING if anything is
already sitting there uncommitted (a leftover fragment from an entirely
UNRELATED earlier interactive session, in this case), and typing then
INSERTS there instead of appending — corrupting old and new text
together. This is what produced the first visibly garbled command
(`load\"/(setq test-verif-marker 424242)' — a splice of an old leftover
test string and the new `(load ...)' payload, submitted as TWO separate
malformed commands because a Return also ended up mid-stream). Reproduced
identically three times before the fix; VERIFYTYPEDLINE correctly
detected the mismatch every time and triggered the dialog/retry path
exactly as designed — the safety net worked, even before its own root
cause (no pre-clear) was fixed.

** Environment flakiness found, NOT fully root-caused: even with
CLEARCONSOLELINE in place, isolated minimal reproductions (clear, type an
8-character chunk, verify) intermittently show NOTHING landing at all
(clipboard read back only the bare prompt) — and separately, fresh
`osascript' invocations that send a click or keystroke (never ones that
only READ state) intermittently raise -25211 (\"not allowed assistive
access\") despite `UI elements enabled' reliably returning true
moments before and after. Both point at an intermittent flakiness in
this session's synthetic-input channel to System Events, not a logic bug
in the script — plausibly related to the sheer volume of synthetic
events sent this session (many hundreds, across dozens of separate
osascript invocations). NOT reproduced as a problem in the very first
Round 6 live runs, which is circumstantial evidence for a
volume/duration-related cause rather than something present from the
start. A genuinely clean/simple ad hoc test (\"HELLOTEST123\", no
chunking, no clear-line, no verify) landed perfectly at the same point in
this investigation, so basic keystroke delivery is not categorically
broken — it is specifically INTERMITTENT.

Also isolated and explained, NOT a production bug: a `'_isoplane'`
command appeared in the console transcript during this round's manual
debugging. Traced to a mistake in an AD HOC DIAGNOSTIC script only —
`keystroke \"e\" using command down' was meant as \"Cmd+End\" (scroll to
bottom) but is actually just Cmd+E, which BricsCAD binds to the isoplane
toggle. The production template never sends this; noted here only so a
future reader does not mistake it for something the template does.

** Net effect: the defensive layers (window-focus check, content
verification, corrupted-input guard, unambiguous dialog handling) are
implemented, logic-reviewed, and CONFIRMED WORKING AS DETECTORS live —
every corruption produced in this round was caught, not silently
submitted, and correctly triggered the recovery/dialog/retry path. What
is NOT yet confirmed live is that a RETRY reliably SUCCEEDS when the
underlying flakiness recurs on the retry attempt too (attemptLimit=3 was
exhausted more than once in this round without a clean success,
consistent with a flakiness that isn't attempt-count-sensitive). Next
session at the machine should: (a) characterize whether the flakiness
correlates with session duration/event volume (a fresh, short session
would test this cleanly), and (b) if it does, consider whether
attemptLimit and/or the giving-up timeouts need to be more generous, or
whether a longer inter-attempt delay lets the channel \"recover\".
